= being there: putting brain, body and world together again  =
[: [[local:zz.texts/clark1997-beingThere.pdf]], https://doi.org/10.7551/mitpress/1552.001.0001 :]

====== short ======
Maybe an influential rejection of representational theories of mind?

====== related to ======
-[[dennett2017-historyOfQualia]]

= main =

== notes on first reading ==
=== preface ===
fp. 10 Representation vs symbolic representation
    But ... a version of the old opposition between matter and mind
    persists. It persists in the way we study brain and mind, excluding as
    "peripheral" the roles of the rest of the body and the local environment.
    It persists in the tradition of modeling intelligence as the production of
    symbolically coded solutions to symbolically expressed puzzles. 

This old opposition is the implicit “bad guy” of the picture which we are
about to overcome. I agree that _symbolic_ representations (i.e. in language or
language like symbols) are not the be-all and and end-all [or even the
foundation] of cognition/reasoning. However, are we setting up to throw the
representational baby out with the symbolic bathwater?

(fp. 11):
    to abandon the idea of neat dividing lines between perception, cognition,
    and action
I think my view aligns neatly with this

=== introduction ===
very first paragraph (fp. 15):
    Where are the artificial minds promised by 1950s science fiction and 1960s
    science journalism? Why are even the best of our "intelligent" artifacts
    still so unspeakably, terminally dumb? One possibility is that we simply
    misconstrued the nature of intelligence itself. We imagined mind as a kind
    of logical reasoning device coupled with a store of explicit data—a kind of
    combination logic machine and filing cabinet.

This has aged, if not poorly, then at least strangely.  It's not just that AI
(or LLMs) have had an unexpected breakthrough. Its that this breakthough has
been achieved by the very disembodied, inactive, purely language focused
approach that Clark spends the next five pages decrying. Clark might argue (and
I might agree) that the flaws of these LLMs (hallucinations, ludicrous errors
etc.,) are ineliminable using current techniques, and are the result of the
language focused approach he is complaining of. He is also right that this
breakthrough was, at least partly, the result of abandoning attempts to encode
logical (or even probabilistic) inferential relations between sentences (and
instead focusing on the statistical relations between linguistic tokens in the
streams produced by competent speakers). Still, if the failures of the old
language-centric attempts to build "intelligent" artifacts counted against that
approach, then surely its successes must count for it.

More specifically, (fp. 17):
    CYC looks set to become a bigger, fancier, but still fundamentally brittle
    and uncomprehending "expert system." Adding more and more knowledge to CYC
    will not remedy this. 

This is kind of the most shocking thing. All the clever people developing clever
theories about minds and cognition didn't get that far. The successes
of LLM's are not, primarily, due to the cleverness of their training algorithms
(though there is room for some). The real driver of their successes is the huge
quantity of data that has been fed into them. Just ”Adding more and more
knowledge”, more and more strings of symbols, *succeeded* in
producing flexible, responsive and often reasonable conversational partners.

=== 1.3 minds without models ===

So there are at least two distinct themes (“morals”) that Clark extracts
from the parade of robots that takes up the beginning of the books body:

The first is lack of symbolic representation, the second is the lack of a
“central planner”. Clark explicitly links the two:

    The trouble with the central planner is that is profoundly impractical. It
    introduces what Rodney Brooks aptly termed a "representational bottleneck"
    blocking fast, real-time response. The reason is that the incoming sensory
    information must be converted into a single symbolic code so that such a
    planner can deal with it. And the planners' output will itself have to be
    converted from its propriety code into the various formats needed to control
    various types of motor response. These steps of translation are time-
    consuming and expensive.

==== lack of central planning ====
The lack of central planning, but rather a range of competing (or at least
interacting) subsystems, is very very reminiscent of Dennett's account of mental
representational processes (see [[dennett2017-historyOfQualia]] and perhaps
[[dennett1991-consciousnessExplained]]).

